import{_ as t,o,c as d,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Setup reports: Windows with Codex Desktop, a Linux sandbox and a security warning","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/install-feedback-episode.md","filePath":"records/install-feedback-episode.md"}'),n={name:"records/install-feedback-episode.md"};function a(r,e,i,c,h,l){return o(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="setup-reports-windows-with-codex-desktop-a-linux-sandbox-and-a-security-warning" tabindex="-1">Setup reports: Windows with Codex Desktop, a Linux sandbox and a security warning <a class="header-anchor" href="#setup-reports-windows-with-codex-desktop-a-linux-sandbox-and-a-security-warning" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Setup reports: Windows with Codex Desktop, a Linux sandbox and a security warning&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Updated 2026-11-09. Three reports about the setup page arrived on 2026-11-08 and 2026-11-09: <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/issues/114" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#114</a>, a first install by Codex Desktop on Windows 11 that stopped after <code>npm install</code>; <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/issues/115" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#115</a>, an install by another client in a Linux sandbox whose service had gone 12 hours later; and the user&#39;s report that Huorong (火绒) frequently flags a <code>Start-Rho.ps1</code> as <code>HEUR:TrojanDownloader/PS.NetLoader.ae</code>. The user asked how to resolve them. This episode traces each item to its owner, changes Rho, and follows the page again.</p><h2 id="the-security-warning" tabindex="-1">The security warning <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-security-warning" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The security warning&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The name is Huorong&#39;s heuristic class for a PowerShell network loader, not a signature match. The page caused it. From Git Bash it told an Agent to write each PowerShell block to a <code>.ps1</code> file and run it with <code>powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File</code>; one block downloaded the Skill&#39;s eight files with <code>Invoke-WebRequest</code>, another removed a log, started <code>cmd.exe</code> with <code>Start-Process -WindowStyle Hidden</code> and polled the log. A script that bypasses the execution policy, fetches files and starts a hidden process is what a PowerShell downloader looks like. Each Agent wrote its own file, so every user&#39;s had different bytes and a different hash, and no reputation or allow-list could form: hence frequent rather than occasional.</p><p>A signature or allow-list would not fix that. The page now has no script: Rho itself does what the script did, and an Agent runs plain <code>npm.cmd</code> and <code>rho.cmd</code> commands.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>The script did</th><th>Now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File</code> on a written <code>.ps1</code></td><td>Nothing to write; the page says not to write the steps into a file or change an execution policy</td></tr><tr><td><code>Invoke-WebRequest</code> of eight Skill files</td><td><code>rho --install-skill DIR</code> copies the Skill from the installed package</td></tr><tr><td><code>Start-Process cmd.exe -WindowStyle Hidden</code>, the log removed first, a polling loop</td><td><code>rho … --background</code></td></tr><tr><td>A loop reading <code>connection.json</code> and requesting the address</td><td><code>rho --status</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>This has not been tried against Huorong: there is no Windows machine or Huorong here.</p><h2 id="the-two-issues-item-by-item" tabindex="-1">The two issues, item by item <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-two-issues-item-by-item" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The two issues, item by item&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Report</th><th>Cause</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>#114.1 <code>node -v</code> passed, then <code>npm</code> was missing</td><td>Codex Desktop&#39;s own Node, with no npm; step 1 checked only <code>node -v</code></td><td>Step 1 checks <code>npm -v</code> and <code>npm prefix -g</code>. No npm: say Rho needs Node with npm, and stop; never fetch npm into another program&#39;s folder. A prefix inside your own app&#39;s runtime (<code>.cache/codex-runtimes</code>): ask first</td></tr><tr><td>#114.2 the Agent assumed <code>%APPDATA%\\npm</code>, cleaned up and reinstalled; <code>PATH</code> changes did not reach its shells</td><td>The page gave no way to find the command folder; a changed <code>PATH</code> reaches only programs started later</td><td>Step 3 names the folder (<code>$(npm prefix -g)/bin</code>, or <code>npm prefix -g</code> on Windows), says to use the full path and tell the person to restart their app, and not to install again</td></tr><tr><td>#114.3 the Windows start had no <code>--agent-backend codex-acp</code></td><td>Since #113 it was named only in prose after the block</td><td>Steps 4 and Windows 4 give Claude Code&#39;s and Codex&#39;s commands in full; step 6 gives <code>--agent</code></td></tr><tr><td>#114.4 <code>codex mcp list</code>: <code>failed to resolve CODEX_HOME</code></td><td>Codex&#39;s CLI in the app&#39;s shell could not find its home folder; not Rho&#39;s</td><td>Step 6 first checks that the client reads its own configuration; when it cannot, the Agent does not set <code>HOME</code> or <code>CODEX_HOME</code> system-wide but gives the person the command for their own terminal. Not reproduced: no Codex Desktop on Windows here</td></tr><tr><td>#114.5 a reading tool refused <code>text/markdown</code></td><td>GitHub Pages serves <code>setup.md</code> as Markdown</td><td>Not changed. The page&#39;s first lines give <code>curl.exe</code> for Windows PowerShell</td></tr><tr><td>#114.6 run the whole path on Windows</td><td>—</td><td>CI&#39;s installed check now runs the page&#39;s commands through <code>rho.cmd</code> in a folder whose name has spaces and Chinese; Codex Desktop is not run</td></tr><tr><td>#115 P1-1 47 minutes waiting on Node 22.13.1</td><td>Step 1 stopped on anything below 22.22. That floor is the tested baseline (Node 22.22.3 on the development Mac), not an API Rho needs: the suite also passed on 18.20.8 (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/example-project-episode">example project</a>)</td><td>A Node 22 below 22.22 is reported as untested and the Agent goes on; below 22 or from 27 it still stops. <code>engines</code> is unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>#115 P1-2 service gone after 12 h, the log only its ready line</td><td>A service ended from outside (killed, out of memory, its session or sandbox gone) writes nothing; a stop in order writes <code>{&quot;shutdown&quot;:…}</code>, a crash its error</td><td><code>rho --status</code>; the shutdown line names <code>by</code> and <code>at</code>; a start keeps the last log; the page says what ends a background Rho and that a remote <code>127.0.0.1</code> is not the person&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td>#115 P2-3 the other-client branch left <code>.mcp.json</code> fields to guesswork</td><td><code>--grant --session</code> printed bare JSON</td><td>Step 6 sends other clients to <code>rho connect</code>, whose reply names the server, its command and arguments, ready entries for four clients, and the <code>rho_index</code> check. No generic <code>mcpServers</code> entry: no such client was checked</td></tr><tr><td>#115 P2-4 <code>--open</code> in a sandbox said nothing</td><td><code>xdg-open</code> with no display failed silently</td><td>On Linux with no <code>DISPLAY</code>, <code>WAYLAND_DISPLAY</code> or WSL, <code>--open</code> opens nothing and says so; an opener that exits non-zero is reported</td></tr><tr><td>#115 P2-5 the grant reply shows the credential&#39;s path</td><td>It does, by design: it is the configuration</td><td>The page and Skill say to configure from it and report the project and server name instead of pasting it</td></tr><tr><td>#115 P2-6 the Skill&#39;s integrity</td><td>Fetched file by file from the site, unrelated to the installed version</td><td>The Skill ships in the package (<code>skills/rho</code>), so npm&#39;s integrity check and the package&#39;s provenance cover it and it matches the installed version. No separate checksum list</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="changes" tabindex="-1">Changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rho</strong> (<code>src/background.ts</code>, <code>src/cli.ts</code>, <code>src/connect-cli.ts</code>):</p><ul><li><code>--background</code> added to a start runs the same start detached with its output in <code>&lt;state&gt;/rho.log</code>, renames the last log to <code>rho.previous.log</code>, and returns once the ready line appears, printing it first, then the process, the log and how to check and stop it. A start that ends first prints its output and exit code; a state already running is refused before anything starts. On macOS and Linux the service gets its own session. On Windows it runs under <code>cmd.exe /d /s /c &quot;… &gt; log 2&gt;&amp;1&quot;</code>, started with no window and no inherited handles: <code>cmd</code> gets a console that shows no window, which the service and the console programs it starts share, and the service is outside the job that ends <code>cmd</code> when the command returns. Arguments containing <code>&quot;</code> or <code>%</code> are refused there.</li><li><code>--status [--state-dir]</code>: running or not, address, process, whether the address answers, the log, a note on how the last service ended, and the other services announced on this machine.</li><li><code>--install-skill DIR</code> copies <code>skills/rho</code> from the package, refusing a folder that holds another skill; <code>--version</code>.</li><li>The <code>{&quot;shutdown&quot;:…}</code> line adds <code>by</code> (<code>rho --stop</code>, <code>SIGTERM</code>, <code>SIGINT</code>, <code>SIGBREAK</code>) and <code>at</code>.</li><li><code>--open</code> reports a missing display and a failed opener.</li><li><code>rho connect</code> treated one state folder named through a link as two services and refused (&quot;More than one running Rho…&quot;). Found below; it now compares real paths.</li></ul><p><strong>Pages:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/blob/main/docs/../SETUP.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">SETUP.md</a> uses these commands and has no script on any system; the Skill&#39;s install, troubleshooting and connect references follow it; the site build no longer requires the page to list the Skill&#39;s files; <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/headless">Headless</a> and the documentation&#39;s start page describe the commands.</p><p><strong>Checks:</strong> <code>launcher-background.test.mjs</code> starts, checks, refuses a second start, stops, restarts, kills from outside and fails a start, with <code>--open</code> on a Linux runner with no display; it also installs the Skill over an earlier copy and next to another skill. A command that held its caller&#39;s output open would time out there. The connect test names one state through a link and fails without the change. CI&#39;s installed check runs <code>--version</code>, <code>--install-skill</code>, <code>--background</code>, <code>--status</code> and <code>--stop</code> through the installed <code>rho</code> and <code>rho.cmd</code>. The packer requires <code>skills/rho/SKILL.md</code>.</p><h2 id="the-run" tabindex="-1">The run <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>macOS arm64, Node 22.22.3 and npm 10.9.8, Claude Code 2.1.319, R 4.5.2, in a throwaway home with <code>rho</code> pointing at this checkout&#39;s build (the commands are not on npm yet), following the page as written except <code>--open</code>, so no tab opened on the user&#39;s screen.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>npm prefix -g</code> and <code>node -p process.execPath</code> were under <code>~/.hermes/node</code>: this Mac&#39;s Node and npm came with another program. The first wording stopped on any folder inside another program, so it would have stopped here, as #115 did; it now stops only without npm and asks only for the Agent app&#39;s own runtime</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Both <code>rho --status</code> lines: &quot;No Rho has run with this state folder.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Returned in 0.8 s with the ready line; <code>--status</code> in a separate command: running, <code>200</code>; the example was copied</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>{&quot;skill&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;rho&quot;,…}}</code> with eight files</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>claude mcp list</code> answered; <code>--grant --agent claude-code</code> added <code>rho-pa14-quorum-sensing</code> for the folder; <code>claude mcp list</code> there: ✔ Connected. A new <code>claude -p</code> session in the folder (model <code>westlakehpc/glm-5.3-flash</code>, only <code>rho_index</code> allowed) called it and answered the granted project id in 28.6 s. <code>rho connect … --as trial-agent</code> refused: &quot;More than one running Rho&quot; named the state as <code>/private/tmp/…</code> and <code>/tmp/…</code>. Fixed, it printed the steps</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>TRUE</code>; <code>missing: car DESeq2</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>rho --stop</code> printed <code>{&quot;stopped&quot;:…}</code>; <code>--status</code>: &quot;Not running. It stopped in order on rho --stop at 2026-11-09T15:30:56.541Z.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Before that, by hand: a failed start (no projects folder) printed Rho&#39;s <code>ENOENT</code> and exited 1 in 0.5 s; a second start was refused with the address; after <code>kill -9</code> the status note said the log ends without a shutdown line; a restart kept the stop&#39;s log as <code>rho.previous.log</code>.</p><p>The suite on macOS: 340 of 343, 3 skipped (Windows only), after one test that required the page to list the Skill&#39;s files was changed to require the package to ship them. <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/actions/runs/42646616217" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CI run 42646616217</a> on <code>511309a6</code> passed every job once its macOS Core build, cancelled before it started, was run again: the suite on macOS, Linux and Windows, and the installed check, with <code>--version</code>, <code>--install-skill</code>, <code>--background</code>, <code>--status</code> and <code>--stop</code> through the installed <code>rho</code> and <code>rho.cmd</code>, on macOS arm64, Linux arm64, Linux x64 with Node 22, 24 and 26, and Windows x64.</p><h2 id="not-done" tabindex="-1">Not done <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-done" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not done&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>No Windows machine, Huorong or Codex Desktop here. Whether Huorong stops flagging, and whether Codex Desktop&#39;s sandbox lets a background Rho outlive the command, are unobserved.</li><li>The page goes to the site with the next npm release: its steps 4 and 5 need commands that 0.5.7 lacks. The front page&#39;s terminal fragment is the 0.5.3 run of the old steps and is recorded again then.</li><li><code>setup.md</code> is still served as <code>text/markdown</code>.</li><li>The Linux sandbox&#39;s 12-hour end is not reproduced; <code>--status</code> can only say that the log has no shutdown line. Rho gives no service-manager unit for keeping it running across restarts.</li><li>No pre-install diagnosis command: Rho cannot run before npm has installed it, so those checks stay in step 1.</li></ul>',22)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
